  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    Qingfu'spire is an Aurora Stack political council founded during the Frozen Peace by Summit-Lord Yahailong and the pioneer Hainou mewei'Frostglass. Its governing body, the Council of Luminaries, operates by aurora-light consensus — formal decisions require clear-sky conditions and visible aurora, a constraint that functions as both legitimizing ritual and structural vulnerability. The faction controls the Promenade Ward, its original beacon-city settlement, along with The Veil Vents and The Aerie Perch, both seized from prior holders. It practices Soar Helm and The Mechanism Refine, the latter a precise aurora-channeling technique used to restore fractured fire-cores.
    
    The summary is accurate: Qingfu'spire consolidated surface settlements through patience, mysticism, and alliances that made their expansion appear inevitable. It only appears inevitable, of course, because the failed expansions are not commemorated.
    
    ## Territorial Disputes
    
    Qingfu'spire claims jurisdiction over Nightshelf tunnel sectors including ☽'dwell Teneanemus~, an ancient deep-carved chamber beneath Wane☽ Oncea. This claim has been contested by the Shadow Covens since before the Orca Incursion ended, producing stretches of tunnel that neither faction effectively monitors — a gap in authority that the chronicle "Forty-Seven Days of Margin" documents with uncomfortable specificity. The Aerie Perch still bears scorch-burns and equipment gouges from its takeover, damage that no one has repaired. Whether this reflects neglect or deliberate memorialization is unclear; the faction's internal records do not comment.
    
    ## The Schism
    
    The faction's defining fracture came when Plunge-Caller Oeichowei, a tide-touched Storm-Caller whom Qingfu'spire had sponsored, broke away to form The Pinnacle, a splinter faction. She took with her the operatives known as The faceless Xi and Cold-flip Qing. The western galleries retained by Qingfu'spire house superior crystal arrays and better aurora exposure — a material advantage maintained through continued investment that stands in visible contrast to the Pinnacle's diminished holdings. Hardliners advocate reclamation. Pragmatists note, correctly, that the faction's alliances with the merchant coalition E'frostlings and the Holdborn of ice-thresh have outlasted most territorial claims won by force.
    
    ## The Radiant Ring and Its Disposition
    
    The Council of Luminaries formally claimed the Radiant Ring — an aurora-glass war hammer carried by the fallen defender Netemia and cracked through use — at a Circle Gathering at the Still Pools. They presented forge-signatures and lineage charts asserting the weapon as Stack property held improperly by the Nightshelf. A lead Luminary, upon examining the artifact, conceded it to Echo∴eliquiae, a Nightshelf memorial hall. Whether this constituted restitution or surrender remained a subject of merchant dispute. The crack in the Ring was widening. One suspects the Luminary's judgment was practical as much as principled.
    
    ## Succession Crisis
    
    The death of Luminary Meridian-of-the-First-Light left the Council unable to convene. The aurora was diminishing. Ice-readers reported uncontrolled visions at increasing frequency. Two claimants — Prism-Ascending and Depth-Keeper — competed for investiture the remaining Luminaries could not grant. Civic functions requiring Luminary authority defaulted to Flipper Accord provisions administered by officials such as Zhachelang zhihi'Opalum, organizer-of-record for the Tide Custom. Fire-core rationing fell to Third Tier. The Long Dark approached.
    
    The faction survives. It has survived raids by Collective☽ Of Led∴merch, a Nightshelf-origin merchant guild. It has survived its own schism. Whether it survives a governance model that requires clear skies during a period of increasing darkness is a question the aurora has not yet answered.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
